menu "espOS health"

    config ESPOS_HEALTH_MAX_CONDITIONS
        int "Distinct condition keys"
        default 12
        range 5 32
        help
            Slots for remembered conditions, one per key (~128 bytes each,
            reserved for the life of the firmware). The set of conditions a
            firmware can raise is fixed at build time, so size this to what
            it actually raises; further keys are rejected with ESP_ERR_NO_MEM.
            espOS itself uses five: lowMemory, memoryTrough, taskStalled,
            netDown and skLinkStalled, which is why the minimum is 5 rather
            than 1. A table too small to hold them drops one silently, and if
            the dropped key is a fatal one the device stops restarting on a
            condition that should restart it. Raise this for a firmware that
            adds keys of its own.

    config ESPOS_HEALTH_MAX_SINKS
        int "Health sinks"
        default 4
        range 1 8
        help
            How many consumers may register for condition changes. espos_sk
            takes one when SignalK notifications are enabled; an application
            wanting a status LED or a line on a display takes another.

    menu "Watchdog policy"

        config ESPOS_HEALTH_POLICY_TICK_S
            int "Policy tick (seconds)"
            default 10
            range 1 60
            help
                How often the policy reads the heap, re-evaluates the built-in
                conditions and counts strikes. With the default strikes below,
                a fatal condition has to be held for tick × strikes = 30 s
                before the device restarts.

        config ESPOS_HEALTH_POLICY_STRIKES
            int "Consecutive fatal ticks before a restart"
            default 3
            range 1 10
            help
                A condition raised with ESPOS_HEALTH_F_REBOOT_ON_ALARM must be
                in ALARM on this many consecutive ticks for the device to
                restart; one tick without it resets the count. A warning or
                an ALARM without the flag never restarts anything.

        config ESPOS_HEALTH_HEAP_WARN_KB
            int "lowMemory: warn below total free heap (KB)"
            default 40
            range 0 4096
            help
                Warning only. 0 disables the check.

        config ESPOS_HEALTH_INTERNAL_WARN_KB
            int "lowMemory: warn below free internal RAM (KB)"
            default 20
            range 0 4096
            help
                Internal RAM is judged separately from the total because on a
                board with PSRAM it is the scarce pool: the radio, DMA and
                every task stack come from it, and tens of megabytes free
                overall can hide its exhaustion. Warning only; 0 disables.

        config ESPOS_HEALTH_INTERNAL_ALARM_KB
            int "lowMemory: fatal alarm below free internal RAM (KB)"
            default 12
            range 0 4096
            help
                Below this the device is about to lose the radio or a task
                stack, and a restart is the recovery. Held for the strike
                window it restarts the device. 0 disables.

                Sits below the deepest legitimate trough seen so far: an
                ESP32-P4 panel measured ~24 KB free / ~23 KB largest block
                while esp-sr's wake pipeline allocates at start, settling
                near 95 KB afterwards. A threshold above that trough would
                restart the panel on every boot.

        config ESPOS_HEALTH_INTERNAL_TROUGH_WARN_KB
            int "memoryTrough: warn if the internal-RAM low-water mark is below (KB)"
            default 10
            range 0 4096
            help
                Warning only, never fatal, and reported as its own condition
                (`memoryTrough`) rather than folded into `lowMemory`.

                The checks above read the free size on a tick, so a trough
                BETWEEN two ticks is invisible to them -- and a transient
                trough is what precedes an allocation failure. A Waveshare
                ESP32-C5 running the BLE gateway measured 27604 B free after
                27 hours with a low-water mark of 4924 B, and 148 B on a later
                run. No tick came near either: each read a free size well
                above the fatal floor. The mark is a sum of per-region
                floors, so it does not prove the TOTAL ever got that low --
                but the parts sum to it, so every internal region had been
                within 148 B of exhaustion at its own worst moment, and an
                allocation fails on a region, not on the total.

                Default 10, below the 12 KB fatal floor: a device that got
                close enough to restarting to be worth mentioning, but not
                so close that the live alarm would already have fired. Raise
                it to hear about a smaller dip; 0 disables.

                Never fatal by construction. A low-water mark does not
                recover within a boot, so a fatal condition on it would
                reboot, see the same mark on the next boot's first dip, and
                reboot again.

        config ESPOS_HEALTH_LARGEST_BLOCK_ALARM_KB
            int "lowMemory: fatal alarm below largest free internal block (KB)"
            default 8
            range 0 4096
            help
                Fragmentation check: the free total can look fine while no
                single block is left for a socket buffer or a task stack.
                Same trough as above applies (~23 KB largest block seen). 0
                disables.

    endmenu

endmenu
